WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:06.060
About 10 miles outside China’s&nbsp;
eleventh-largest city lies a graveyard.

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:14.040
Its occupants aren’t human. Nor are they&nbsp;
dead, though they have seen better days.

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:21.840
These hundreds and hundreds of little&nbsp;
white rectangles are cars. Electric cars.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:27.900
…Victims of China’s incredibly rapid&nbsp;
and unparalleled automotive revolution.

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:35.400
When this Changan Eado debuted in 2015&nbsp;
with a range of just over 120-miles and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:40.440
a top speed of less than 90 miles an hour,&nbsp;
it was among the country’s best-selling EVs.

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:45.960
At the time, China exported only about 375,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:52.680
cars a year, less than India or the United&nbsp;
States, and a tiny fraction of Japan or Germany.

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:57.300
For the next five years, it&nbsp;
made only modest progress.

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:03.720
Then, while the rest of the world was&nbsp;
stuck indoors, China leaped ahead of India,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:10.320
followed by the U.S., and is on track to beat&nbsp;
both Germany and Japan to become #1 this year.

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:18.480
Today, the Changan Eado is but a forgotten&nbsp;
relic – an iPod – functional yet worthless.

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:23.760
In its place, Chinese drivers have&nbsp;
hundreds of models to choose from.

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:30.660
There’s the BYD Han – a Tesla Model S in&nbsp;
form and function for nearly half the price.

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:37.740
There’s the Wuling Bingo – an innovative hatchback&nbsp;
equipped with a built-in inflatable mattress.

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:45.120
There’s the best-selling Hongguang Mini –&nbsp;
a $5,000 4-seat, street-legal micro-car.

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:51.360
And for those decidedly not on a budget,&nbsp;
the $700,000 Rolls-Royce of China:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:56.520
the Hongqi L5 – proudly used&nbsp;
by none other than Xi Jinping.

00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:03.600
How did China go from 500 EVs on the&nbsp;
streets of Shanghai in 2011 to being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:06.600
the undisputed leader in electric cars today?

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:13.620
Sponsored by Ground News – the platform&nbsp;
where you can easily compare news sources,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:17.460
protect against media bias,&nbsp;
and stay on top of headlines.

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:24.060
This is what China’s most populous&nbsp;
city looked like in April 2000.

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:29.940
You’ll notice lots of bikes, quite&nbsp;
a few scooters, and even some buses.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:33.540
What you don’t see a lot of are cars.

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:40.140
Just four million were on the road&nbsp;
for a population of 1.3 billion – a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:44.220
number surpassed by the United&nbsp;
States somewhere around 1910.

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:51.180
But by 2005, car ownership had more than&nbsp;
tripled and its streets looked like this.

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:55.320
Suddenly, China had an oil problem.

00:02:56.520 --> 00:02:59.820
The basic challenge can be&nbsp;
boiled down to just two numbers:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:06.900
China consumes upwards of 13% of the&nbsp;
world’s oil yet is home to a mere 1.5%.

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:11.220
The rest it mainly imports from the Middle East.

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:18.180
Not only does this make it incredibly dependent&nbsp;
on a small handful of countries, but because the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:24.180
vast majority of this oil arrives on tankers, it&nbsp;
also depends on the generosity of the U.S. Navy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:29.940
to patrol these waters, not to mention the&nbsp;
goodwill of every nation along this route.

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:33.720
…Needless to say: not ideal.

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:37.800
China also had a pollution problem.

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:43.620
To this day more of its citizens die of&nbsp;
pollution each year than live in Hawaii.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:48.060
That’s well over 4,000 deaths on the average day.

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:53.580
While the country is no stranger to&nbsp;
environmental destruction, these costs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:58.380
are typically borne disproportionately&nbsp;
by the less socioeconomically advantaged.

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:01.980
In other words, nickel mines, coal plants,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:05.700
and mega-dams are built where&nbsp;
residents are powerless to stop them.

00:04:06.540 --> 00:04:12.660
Air pollution, by contrast, is often worse in&nbsp;
its most populous and wealthy coastal cities.

00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:17.820
According to one estimate, 38%&nbsp;
of the Chinese population lives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:22.080
in an area where the average air quality&nbsp;
would be deemed “unhealthy” by the EPA.

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:28.380
Many of 21st Century China’s largest&nbsp;
protests have revolved around air pollution.

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:31.920
Enter: electric vehicles.

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:36.900
Beijing calculated that nearly&nbsp;
a third of its smog was caused&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:41.640
by cars. And that even if all its&nbsp;
electricity were sourced from coal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:45.960
EVs would still generate fewer emissions&nbsp;
than internal combustion engines.

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:50.400
For years, China had been&nbsp;
trying to catch up to German&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:54.540
and Japanese automakers by forcing&nbsp;
them to work with local companies.

00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:57.900
Except, rather than stealing from their foreign&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.900 --> 00:05:03.180
counterparts – as is often imagined –&nbsp;
Chinese engineers became dependent on them.

00:05:04.140 --> 00:05:08.700
Eventually, the Chinese government&nbsp;
realized it simply wasn’t going to win.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:16.020
So, it decided to start a new race. One&nbsp;
in which its competitors’ head start was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.020 --> 00:05:21.600
actually a disadvantage – Mercedes&nbsp;
and Toyota and Ford were complacent.

00:05:22.620 --> 00:05:27.720
Unlike traditional cars, EVs were&nbsp;
perfectly compatible with China’s economy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:33.240
Where internal combustion engines&nbsp;
rely on small amounts of high-skill,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:40.080
high-cost labor, EVs rely on relatively&nbsp;
larger amounts of low-skill, low-cost labor.

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:45.840
By value, an electric car is essentially&nbsp;
a battery wrapped in some packaging.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:50.580
And batteries – to grossly&nbsp;
oversimplify – are commodities.

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:55.500
Like any commodity, the cost would&nbsp;
inevitably go down with scale.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.340 --> 00:06:00.600
The challenge was sustaining these&nbsp;
companies until they reached profitability.

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:04.980
China’s solution was to just buy from them.

00:06:05.700 --> 00:06:10.020
After the 2008 financial crisis,&nbsp;
Chinese cities helped stimulate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.020 --> 00:06:14.280
the economy by buying loads and&nbsp;
loads of electric buses and taxis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.060 --> 00:06:23.700
By 2014, Shenzhen had more than 1,200. Three years&nbsp;
later, all 17,000 of its buses were electric.

00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:30.600
Then came the subsidies. Billions&nbsp;
and billions worth of subsidies.

00:06:31.500 --> 00:06:36.780
At one point, buyers could save as&nbsp;
much as $16,000 U.S. Dollars per EV.

00:06:37.860 --> 00:06:42.660
But although the Chinese government may have&nbsp;
handed out as much as a hundred billion dollars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.660 --> 00:06:46.800
these subsidies weren’t the biggest&nbsp;
reason people bought electric cars.

00:06:47.580 --> 00:06:51.960
In a 2016 survey, 52% of respondents said they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:55.320
would not have purchased their&nbsp;
EV if not for its license plate.

00:06:56.100 --> 00:07:04.320
Most Chinese cities distribute new license plates&nbsp;
by lottery, auction, or both. In 2016, your odds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:13.020
of winning the Beijing lottery were one in 667.&nbsp;
In Shanghai, plates can sell for $10,000 or more.

00:07:13.860 --> 00:07:18.540
And even with plates, you may only be&nbsp;
allowed to drive on certain days of the week.

00:07:19.500 --> 00:07:24.600
Buy an EV, on the other hand, and you’ll&nbsp;
automatically receive a special green license&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:32.640
plate – no lottery or auction necessary. Green&nbsp;
plates are China’s American Express Platinum – a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:38.400
mass-market golden ticket to drive on any day&nbsp;
of the week and, in some places, park for free.

00:07:39.660 --> 00:07:44.460
Unsurprisingly, these policies&nbsp;
worked. A little too well.

00:07:45.300 --> 00:07:49.860
The country became saturated with&nbsp;
hundreds of nearly identical brands.

00:07:50.820 --> 00:07:54.240
It wasn’t so much that China&nbsp;
was propping-up uncompetitive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.240 --> 00:07:59.520
companies as hundreds of cities were each&nbsp;
propping-up their uncompetitive companies.

00:08:00.300 --> 00:08:06.120
It was as if Seattle, Minneapolis, Houston, and&nbsp;
Pittsburg each had its own Tesla competitor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.840 --> 00:08:09.360
Rather than let the best EV win,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:14.340
each region was too dependent on its&nbsp;
“local champion” to allow its failure.

00:08:15.420 --> 00:08:18.480
On one hand, this unusually saturated market&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.480 --> 00:08:22.440
means Chinese consumers have more&nbsp;
choices than any other country.

00:08:23.760 --> 00:08:27.240
Take a look, for instance, at EV sales in America.

00:08:28.380 --> 00:08:33.060
At first glance, this may look&nbsp;
like success. But if we remove&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.060 --> 00:08:39.540
the largest company, you can see that EVs&nbsp;
haven’t really grown over time. Tesla has.

00:08:40.380 --> 00:08:45.300
Americans effectively have four models&nbsp;
from one major brand to choose from.

00:08:46.260 --> 00:08:52.320
By contrast, Chinese companies released&nbsp;
107 new models last year alone – from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.320 --> 00:08:56.460
ultra-affordable “mini EVs” to&nbsp;
ones where you can quickly swap&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.460 --> 00:09:00.600
out your battery at one of thousands of&nbsp;
stations instead of waiting for a charge.

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:08.460
On the other hand, five hundred companies is just&nbsp;
too many – even for a country as large as China.

00:09:09.660 --> 00:09:14.700
So, around 2016, the Communist Party&nbsp;
did something uncharacteristic:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.700 --> 00:09:17.160
it let Capitalism take the wheel.

00:09:18.180 --> 00:09:21.000
Subsidies have been slowly phased out and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:24.600
local governments prohibited from&nbsp;
favoring one company over another.

00:09:25.980 --> 00:09:30.840
At the same time, Beijing dropped a&nbsp;
nuclear bomb on the Chinese auto industry.

00:09:31.620 --> 00:09:34.980
After decades of carefully&nbsp;
nurturing local companies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.980 --> 00:09:39.000
the Chinese government rolled out the&nbsp;
red carpet for a foreign competitor.

00:09:39.900 --> 00:09:44.520
For the first time ever, Tesla was&nbsp;
given permission to build its 9 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.520 --> 00:09:48.960
square foot Shanghai factory without&nbsp;
working with a joint Chinese partner.

00:09:49.740 --> 00:09:51.060
And that’s not all.

00:09:51.780 --> 00:09:58.680
Inspired by California, Beijing instituted&nbsp;
a credit system. Every automaker is now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.680 --> 00:10:03.300
required to sell a certain number of EVs&nbsp;
for each conventional car it produces.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:09.120
Those who fall short must make up the difference&nbsp;
by purchasing credits from their competitors.

00:10:10.380 --> 00:10:16.980
Since Tesla only produces EVs, it earned&nbsp;
nearly $1.6 billion from selling credits in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.980 --> 00:10:24.840
2020 alone. Without this revenue, the company&nbsp;
would’ve lost $859 million overall that year.

00:10:25.980 --> 00:10:29.640
One could argue that Tesla&nbsp;
was the largest beneficiary of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.640 --> 00:10:32.400
Chinese state involvement in the entire industry.

00:10:33.300 --> 00:10:36.900
Protectionism? Sure, for an American company.

00:10:38.580 --> 00:10:44.100
Chinese companies were suddenly on&nbsp;
their own. Meanwhile, here was this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.100 --> 00:10:48.720
800-pound gorilla with a stronger&nbsp;
brand than all the rest combined.

00:10:48.720 --> 00:10:51.960
To make matters worse, Tesla,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.960 --> 00:10:57.180
still on the high-end, lowered the cost of&nbsp;
its two most popular models by 6 and 13%.

00:10:59.100 --> 00:11:01.920
What happened next was an all-out price war.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.760 --> 00:11:09.420
In the space of just a few short years, something&nbsp;
like 80% of China’s EV companies collapsed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.200 --> 00:11:16.500
NIO, one of its largest, lost&nbsp;
$131,000 for every car it sold.

00:11:17.520 --> 00:11:22.800
But this consolidation – painful as it&nbsp;
was – had the effect Beijing intended.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.340 --> 00:11:26.160
Those that survived have thrived.

00:11:27.240 --> 00:11:31.500
Today, about 40% of new car&nbsp;
sales in China are electric.

00:11:32.580 --> 00:11:37.560
Of them, one Chinese company –&nbsp;
BYD – sells more than the 2nd,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.560 --> 00:11:42.120
3rd, 4th, 5th, 6th, and 7th-place brands combined.

00:11:42.900 --> 00:11:45.840
It’s on track to sell more electric cars than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.840 --> 00:11:49.500
Volkswagen will sell cars&nbsp;
– of any kind – this year.

00:11:49.500 --> 00:11:54.660
Tesla is the only foreign carmaker not in decline.

00:11:55.740 --> 00:12:00.780
The days of Ford, Honda, and BMW&nbsp;
dominating the Chinese market are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.780 --> 00:12:04.440
over and the tide is beginning to&nbsp;
flow in the opposite direction.

00:12:04.440 --> 00:12:08.100
Chinese cars can now be found in Mexico,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.100 --> 00:12:14.040
Saudi Arabia, Belgium, the U.K., Chile,&nbsp;
and Australia – to name just a few.

00:12:15.120 --> 00:12:18.900
Meanwhile, at a time when China&nbsp;
is embracing the free market,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.900 --> 00:12:22.380
it’s now America’s turn to&nbsp;
resort to protectionism.

00:12:23.400 --> 00:12:29.700
Previously, nearly every EV buyer in&nbsp;
America could receive a $7,500 tax credit.

00:12:29.700 --> 00:12:33.660
Now, only cars whose batteries were produced in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.660 --> 00:12:38.220
North America and whose minerals were&nbsp;
processed by a U.S. ally are eligible.

00:12:39.540 --> 00:12:43.140
America is trying to out China China.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.980 --> 00:12:48.300
To impose the same restrictions&nbsp;
it once criticized Beijing for.

00:12:49.380 --> 00:12:50.580
But will it work?

00:12:51.480 --> 00:12:53.460
First, consider demand.

00:12:54.060 --> 00:12:57.180
In a recent survey, the average&nbsp;
American said they would need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.180 --> 00:13:02.640
a range of over 500 miles to consider&nbsp;
buying an EV – the most of any country.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.540 --> 00:13:07.860
Chinese consumers had the lowest&nbsp;
expectations – about half as many.

00:13:08.640 --> 00:13:13.200
It makes sense: China has fast and&nbsp;
efficient high-speed trains for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.200 --> 00:13:18.240
longer journeys. For Americans,&nbsp;
car ownership is a way of life.

00:13:19.320 --> 00:13:22.680
Most Chinese consumers are buying their first-ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.680 --> 00:13:28.560
car. They value its interface as much&nbsp;
as, if not more than its performance.

00:13:29.520 --> 00:13:33.540
In other words, Chinese and&nbsp;
American consumers are different.

00:13:34.800 --> 00:13:36.660
Secondly, there’s supply.

00:13:37.500 --> 00:13:40.380
The Chinese government didn’t attempt to manifest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.380 --> 00:13:45.960
a battery industry out of thin air – it&nbsp;
subsidized those that already existed.

00:13:46.680 --> 00:13:51.960
BYD – the country’s largest EV brand,&nbsp;
as you’ll recall – began, in fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.960 --> 00:13:59.340
as a battery maker. It successfully produced phone&nbsp;
batteries for years before refocusing on cars.

00:14:00.300 --> 00:14:07.260
America doesn’t have those companies to&nbsp;
subsidize. It’s effectively starting from scratch.

00:14:08.400 --> 00:14:15.180
Finally, the most effective Chinese policies&nbsp;
were coercive. A Civil War would break out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:15.180 --> 00:14:19.140
before the United States would restrict the&nbsp;
days of the week on which you can drive.

00:14:20.280 --> 00:14:24.300
All of this suggests America&nbsp;
has a tough road ahead.

00:14:25.020 --> 00:14:29.760
…Nevermind that it’s starting this&nbsp;
race about 15-20 years too late.

00:14:30.660 --> 00:14:37.560
As of today, just 25 models of cars are&nbsp;
eligible for the new $7,500 federal tax credit.

00:14:38.280 --> 00:14:44.820
The other 70% of all electric, hydrogen, and&nbsp;
hybrid cars sold in America are ineligible.

00:14:45.780 --> 00:14:50.220
Biden’s Inflation Reduction Act is&nbsp;
an attempt to have it both ways:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:50.220 --> 00:14:57.240
to increase the number of EVs on America’s roads&nbsp;
while simultaneously decreasing China’s dominance.

00:14:58.020 --> 00:15:02.640
But the reality is: China has spent&nbsp;
decades building up its supply chain.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.640 --> 00:15:07.920
Nearly every electric car company in&nbsp;
the world depends on its batteries.

00:15:08.700 --> 00:15:14.880
It refines over half of the world’s lithium and&nbsp;
three-quarters of the world’s nickel and cobalt.

00:15:15.720 --> 00:15:21.720
A world without China is, at least&nbsp;
for now – a world without EVs.

00:15:23.340 --> 00:15:28.080
Nearly 40% of EVs in America are&nbsp;
registered in a single state:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.080 --> 00:15:34.620
California. And one of the reasons for this&nbsp;
incredible polarization is their media coverage.

00:15:34.620 --> 00:15:39.720
In choosing what and, perhaps more&nbsp;
importantly, what not to include,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.720 --> 00:15:46.200
journalists have no choice but to introduce&nbsp;
bias. And although we may be aware of this bias,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.200 --> 00:15:49.680
it’s often invisible until compared, side-by-side,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:49.680 --> 00:15:55.860
with other sources covering the same story –&nbsp;
something few of us have the time or energy to do.

